# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
knows_something	one
knowing	two	knowing	P4	being a knower in respect of some things, as knowing one thing makes you	being a knower absolutely, which would require knowing all things
knows_all	one
ignorant_something	one
# The shift is clearest between S3 and S5. In S3, "knowing" follows from knowing anything at all. In S5, it is taken as entailing omniscience. S4 and S8 show Socrates reading it in the respect-relative sense.
# The respect-relative sense is used in S3 by P2 and in S7 by P7, where not knowing something makes you "not knowing" in that respect. C in S9 is ambiguous: it is a real contradiction only in the absolute sense, but it is true only in the relative sense. I left it with the first sense, though one could list it with P4.
